WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.120
The first film sorted close partnerships by each partner's outcome.

2
00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:08.420
But how can we tell whether a root fungus really helps a plant?

3
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:10.900
We need a fair comparison.

4
00:00:14.483 --> 00:00:16.183
The proposed trade is clear.

5
00:00:17.583 --> 00:00:20.283
Fungus threads bring minerals from soil into roots.

6
00:00:21.463 --> 00:00:23.603
The plant passes food it made to the fungus.

7
00:00:24.903 --> 00:00:28.703
That suggests mutualism, but a suggestion is not a measurement.

8
00:00:29.833 --> 00:00:34.893
Researchers grew Jubilee marigolds with living root fungus and without it.

9
00:00:35.953 --> 00:00:38.173
Matching pots received the same water level.

10
00:00:39.373 --> 00:00:43.613
The fungus was the planned difference, so plant growth could be compared fairly.

11
00:00:44.767 --> 00:00:46.987
The experiment also tested drought.

12
00:00:48.427 --> 00:00:50.547
Less water reduced growth in both groups.

13
00:00:51.727 --> 00:00:56.267
That lets us ask whether the fungus benefit appears across the tested conditions,

14
00:00:56.727 --> 00:00:57.727
not just once.

15
00:00:58.667 --> 00:01:02.847
After growth, the shoots were cut and dried until their water was gone.

16
00:01:04.127 --> 00:01:05.227
Then each shoot was weighed.

17
00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:10.027
Drying means water in the shoots cannot confuse the comparison.

18
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.220
What pattern would support a plant benefit from the fungus?

19
00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.760
Choose before the bars appear: higher dry mass with fungus, equal mass, or lower

20
00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:21.760
mass.

21
00:01:27.217 --> 00:01:31.617
Across the tested water levels, mean shoot mass was higher with fungus.

22
00:01:32.997 --> 00:01:36.277
The paired bars now use the study values shown on screen.

23
00:01:37.577 --> 00:01:39.577
Yet the smallest gap could be due to chance.

24
00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:43.320
Each bar is a mean, an average for a group.

25
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.940
It does not say every plant had that mass.

26
00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.060
Group averages make the planned comparison visible.

27
00:01:52.183 --> 00:01:57.483
Researchers also test whether a gap is larger than expected from chance variation.

28
00:01:58.703 --> 00:02:00.983
The smallest gap did not pass that check.

29
00:02:02.163 --> 00:02:03.703
The other gaps did in this study.

30
00:02:04.783 --> 00:02:09.703
The dry-mass evidence supports a benefit to these marigolds under these greenhouse

31
00:02:09.703 --> 00:02:13.683
conditions. It does not show how many plants lived.

32
00:02:14.863 --> 00:02:17.303
It also does not measure the fungus's gain.

33
00:02:18.433 --> 00:02:22.173
Other evidence shows root fungi receive plant-made food.

34
00:02:23.513 --> 00:02:27.513
Combine that with the plant-growth result, and mutualism fits this case.

35
00:02:28.753 --> 00:02:31.713
The claim stays tied to what was tested and measured.

36
00:02:32.783 --> 00:02:35.743
A label is never permission to assume the outcome.

37
00:02:37.023 --> 00:02:40.563
Another plant, fungus, or condition may give a different result.

38
00:02:41.923 --> 00:02:46.143
Compare partners fairly, measure outcomes, and keep every limit attached.

39
00:02:47.217 --> 00:02:51.577
Start with the exchange, then ask what the evidence supports for each partner.

40
00:02:52.817 --> 00:02:57.777
That turns help, harm, or no measured effect into a testable scientific claim.
